    "A computer monitor displays a lateral-view knee radiograph. The distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The patellofemoral joint space is visible on this lateral projection. A small bony projection is visible at the inferior pole of the patella. No laterality marker is legible at this image resolution. No additional radiograph views are visible on screen.",
    "A computer monitor displays a lateral-view knee radiograph. The distal femur, proximal tibia, fibula head, and patella are visible. The patellofemoral joint space is visible on this lateral projection. A small bony spur is visible at the inferior pole of the patella. The joint surfaces of the femoral condyles and tibial plateau are visible; the posterior joint space appears preserved on this view. No compartment-specific joint-space narrowing is clearly measurable from this lateral projection alone. No additional radiograph views (AP standing, sunrise/Merchant) are visible on screen. Patient identification fields are present in the upper left corner of the screen but are not legible at this resolution.",
    "A computer monitor displays a lateral-view knee radiograph in what appears to be a DICOM viewer. The distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible in profile. A small bony projection is visible at the inferior pole of the patella. The joint space between the femoral condyle and tibial plateau is visible. No laterality marker is legible at this image resolution. A dashed vertical reference line is overlaid on the image by the software.",
    "A computer monitor displays a knee radiograph. The view shown is a lateral projection of the knee joint. The distal femur, proximal tibia, fibula head, and patella are visible. A vertical dashed measurement line is overlaid on the right portion of the image. The inferior pole of the patella shows a small bony projection. The joint space between the femoral condyles and tibial plateau is visible on this lateral view. No laterality marker is legible at this image resolution.",
    "A computer monitor displays a lateral-view knee radiograph. The distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The patella is seen in profile. A small bony projection is visible at the inferior pole of the patella. The joint space between the femoral condyle and tibial plateau is visible on this lateral projection. No laterality marker is legible at this image resolution. No AP or sunrise/Merchant views are visible in this frame.",
    "A computer monitor displays two knee radiograph views side by side. The left panel shows a lateral view of a knee; bony cortical margins of the distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The right panel appears to show a second view of the knee (likely AP or a second lateral), though it is partially cut off at the right edge of the screen. A marker \"L\" is visible on the right panel. Joint spaces are visible on both views. On the lateral view, the inferior pole of the patella shows a small bony projection along its inferior cortical margin. Soft tissue outlines are visible without obvious gross swelling on the radiograph. Text overlays are present in the upper-left corner of the display but are not legible at this resolution.",
    "A computer monitor displays a digital radiograph viewer. The main panel shows a lateral view of a knee joint. In the thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen, multiple smaller radiograph thumbnails are visible, appearing to include AP and lateral views of what appear to be two knees side by side. On the large lateral view currently displayed, the distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The patellofemoral joint space is visible on this lateral projection. No laterality marker (R/L) is legible at the image resolution captured. Text overlays are present on the viewer interface but are not legible at this resolution.",
    "A monitor displays a radiograph viewer application. The main panel shows a lateral-view knee X-ray in large format. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen shows multiple smaller radiograph thumbnails, which appear to include additional knee views (consistent with AP and possibly patellofemoral/sunrise projections), though the thumbnails are too small to characterize findings in detail. On the large lateral view, the distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The patellofemoral joint space is visible on this lateral projection. No laterality marker (\"L\" or \"R\") is legible in the main image at this resolution. Patient demographic or label text is visible in the upper-left panel but is not legible at this resolution.",
    "A monitor displays a digital radiograph viewer showing what appears to be a lateral view of a knee. The distal femur, proximal tibia, fibula head, and patella are visible. The patellofemoral joint space is visible on this lateral projection. Thumbnail images are visible in a panel on the left side of the screen, suggesting additional views are available in the series. On-screen text and patient data fields are present but not legible at this resolution. No laterality markers (R/L) are clearly readable in the frame.",
    "A monitor displays a knee radiograph in lateral view. The distal femur, proximal tibia, fibula head, and patella are visible. The patellofemoral joint space is visible on this lateral projection. Bony contours of the femoral condyles and tibial plateau are visible. No laterality marker is legible at the image resolution captured. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen shows at least two additional smaller radiograph images, but their views and details are not resolvable. Patient demographic or study data fields are visible in the upper-left corner of the screen but are not legible at this resolution.",
    "A computer monitor displays a lateral-view knee radiograph. A marker \"R\" is visible on the image, indicating the right knee. The distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The joint space appears preserved. No gross osteophytes, fracture lines, or obvious joint-space narrowing are clearly discernible at this image resolution and viewing angle. A thumbnail panel is visible on the left side of the screen, suggesting at least one additional image is available in the series, but its content is not legible at this resolution.",
    "A computer monitor displays a lateral-view knee radiograph. The marker \"R\" is visible in the lower-left area of the image, indicating this is the right knee. The distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The patellofemoral joint space and tibiofemoral joint space are visible on this lateral projection. No gross fracture lines are apparent at this resolution. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen shows at least one additional radiographic view, but the content of that thumbnail is not sufficiently resolved to describe findings. No osteophytes or joint-space changes can be definitively characterised at the image resolution and angle captured.",
    "A monitor displays a digital radiograph viewer. The main panel shows a lateral view of a knee. The distal femur, proximal tibia, and patella are visible. The joint space appears preserved. No gross osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, or joint-space narrowing are clearly discernible at this image resolution and viewing angle. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen shows additional smaller radiograph images, including what appear to be at least two knee views (one appears to be an AP view and one a lateral), but these thumbnails are too small to characterize findings. No laterality markers (R/L) are legible in the frame.",
    "A PACS-style radiology viewer is displayed on a monitor. The main large image shows a close-up radiographic view of what appears to be a knee joint — specifically a view consistent with a sunrise/Merchant (axial patellofemoral) projection, showing the patella and the trochlear groove. A small \"L\" marker is visible within the image. The articular surfaces of the patellofemoral joint are visible; the joint space appears present. Bony contours of the patella and femoral trochlea are visible without obvious gross deformity at this resolution. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the viewer shows at least one additional smaller radiographic image, but its detail is not resolvable at this image quality.",
    "A monitor displays a PACS or radiograph-viewing application. The large central image shows a close-up radiographic view of what appears to be a patellofemoral (sunrise/Merchant-type) projection, showing the distal femoral trochlear groove and patella in axial orientation. The joint space between the patella and trochlea is visible. A vertical measurement or annotation line is overlaid on the image. On the left panel of the application, a thumbnail strip shows approximately six smaller radiograph thumbnails; the thumbnails appear to include multiple knee views (at least two appear to be AP or PA projections and two appear to be lateral projections, based on their silhouette shapes). Text fields are visible in the upper-left panel but are not legible at this resolution. No laterality markers (R/L) are clearly readable in the frame.",
    "A monitor displays a radiograph viewer application. The large active image appears to be a lateral or oblique view of a knee joint, showing the distal femur and proximal tibia in close-up. The articular surfaces are visible; the joint space appears present. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen shows multiple smaller radiograph thumbnails, including what appear to be AP and lateral knee views on both sides. The main image has a label \"L\" visible near the center-lower portion of the image. Image text and metadata are present in the viewer interface but are not legible at this resolution.",
    "A computer monitor displays a radiographic imaging workstation. The large central panel shows a radiograph of a knee in what appears to be a skyline (axial/sunrise) projection, showing the patella and the underlying trochlear groove. A white letter \"R\" marker is visible on the image, indicating the right side. The left panel of the workstation shows a series of smaller thumbnail radiographic images, consistent with a knee series. The image is moderately zoomed, showing the inferior aspect of the patella and the femoral trochlea in profile. No patient is visible in the frame.",
    "A computer monitor displays a radiographic imaging workstation. The large central image shows what appears to be a skyline (axial/sunrise) view of a patella, with the patellofemoral joint visible. A white letter \"R\" marker is visible in the lower-left area of the central image, indicating this is the right side. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen shows multiple smaller radiographic images of what appear to be knee views in various projections. No patient is directly visible in the frame; only the screen content is present.",
    "A monitor displays a radiographic image consistent with a skyline/sunrise (axial) view of a patella. The patellofemoral joint is visible centrally on screen. The articular contour of the patella appears rounded and smooth at this resolution. The medial and lateral facets are visible. No gross bony fragmentation is discernible. A thumbnail strip of additional images is visible along the left edge of the screen but is too small to characterize. No laterality marker (R/L) is legible in the frame. Image detail is limited by the oblique camera angle and screen glare.",
    "A computer monitor displays a radiographic imaging workstation. The main large panel shows what appears to be a sunrise/Merchant-view (axial) projection of a patellofemoral joint, labeled \"R\" in the image, indicating the right side. The patella is visible centrally within the trochlear groove. A thumbnail panel on the left side of the screen shows a series of smaller radiographic images, consistent with a multi-view knee series (thumbnails appear to include AP and lateral projections). The large active image is angled/oblique to the camera, limiting full assessment of fine detail, but the patellofemoral articulation and trochlear contour are visible without obvious gross asymmetry discernible at this resolution and angle.",
    "A computer monitor displays a radiographic image consistent with a Merchant (sunrise) view of the patellofemoral joint. An \"R\" marker is visible on the image, indicating the right side. The main large image shows the patella seated within the trochlear groove; the trochlear groove and medial and lateral facets of the patellofemoral joint are visible. On the left side of the screen, a thumbnail panel displays several smaller radiographic images, which appear to include additional knee views (AP and/or lateral projections), though their detail is not resolvable at this resolution. Patient demographic and study information text is visible in the upper left of the screen but is not legible.",
    "A computer monitor displays what appears to be a medical imaging viewer interface (teal/white header visible) showing a grid of multiple radiographic images. The images appear to depict lower limb radiographs (ankle and foot regions visible across multiple thumbnail views), displayed in a gallery/grid layout. Individual image labels and metadata text are present but not legible at this resolution. No patient is visible in the frame. The audio context references physiotherapy as a first-line plan.",
    "A computer monitor displays a medical imaging file viewer (appears to be a web-based platform with a teal/turquoise header). The patient name visible at the top left reads \"Leslie Podione.\" The screen shows a \"Files\" section containing a grid of multiple radiographic images — the thumbnails appear to show lower limb radiographs (ankle and foot regions are discernible in several thumbnails). No injection options, MRI requests, or referral documents are visibly legible on screen. The audio context references physiotherapy as a first-line plan, but no written referral or document to that effect is visible on screen.",
    "A monitor displays a medical imaging viewer showing a grid of multiple radiographic images. The thumbnails appear to depict skeletal structures, consistent in appearance with plain radiographs of joints or limbs, though individual images are too small to confirm specific anatomy or laterality. No R/L markers are legible at this resolution. The audio context references physiotherapy as a first-line plan. No injection options, MRI orders, or referral documents are visible on screen.",
